{"name":"Light Topaz Ochre","hex":"#b08971","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"topaz","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A silicate mineral of aluminium and fluorine, usually tinted by impurities.","An often clear, yellowish-brown gemstone cut from this."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellowish-brown color, like that of the gemstone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/topaz"},{"term":"ochre","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A clay earth pigment containing silica, aluminum and ferric oxide.","A somewhat dark yellowish orange colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a yellow-orange colour.","Referring to cultures that covered their dead with ochre."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ochre"}],"entries":[{"title":"Topaz","blocks":[{"text":"Topaz is a silicate mineral made of aluminum and fluorine with the chemical formula Al2SiO4(F, OH)2. It is used as a gemstone in jewelry and other adornments. Common topaz in its natural state is colorless, though trace element impurities can make it pale blue or golden-brown to yellow-orange. Topaz is often treated with heat or radiation to make it a deep blue, reddish-orange, pale green, pink, or purple."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Topaze%2C_quartz_fum%C3%A9_1.jpg/330px-Topaze%2C_quartz_fum%C3%A9_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Topaz","wikidata":"Q134985"},{"title":"Ochre","blocks":[{"text":"Ochre is a family of natural clay earth pigments, made up of ferric oxide and varying amounts of clay and sand, ranging in colour from yellow to deep orange or brown. The term is also used for the colours produced by this pigment, especially a light brownish-yellow. A variant of ochre containing a large amount of hematite, or dehydrated iron oxide, has a reddish tint known as red ochre. The term \"ochre\" is colloquially used to cover a range of different substances used for their colours in Aboriginal Australian art and ceremonial decoration, for instance the clay known as \"white ochre\" or \"pipe clay\" is derived from kaolin or gypsum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Hellocker-_Pigment.JPG/330px-Hellocker-_Pigment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ochre","wikidata":"Q12353401"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Topaz.GIF/500px-Topaz.GIF?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Topaz.GIF/500px-Topaz.GIF?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"